  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nToday started what is expected to be a prolonged stretch of \nquiet and dry weather. The synoptic high pressure system over \nthe south central CONUS finally has some company with a small \ntrough situated over southern Minnesota. The trough however is \nnot forecast to tangibly affect the weather over the forecast \narea. Unlike the last few days, the high pressure extends to the\nsurface, all but ending any precipitation chances for the next \nfew days. Thanks to the cold front and lingering CAA, highs this\nweekend will be a cooler break from the triple digits residing \nin the 90s. Still hot but within what is expected for SW Kansas \nin early August. \n\nBy Monday, the heat returns with highs in the triple digits and \nas high as 107 in the western counties. At this moment, it \nlooks like the hottest day of the stretch. The aspect that \nwarrants monitoring whether the apparent temperatures reach the \n105 mark for Heat Advisory criteria. For the Monday \ntemperatures, the hottest areas flirt with the line. After \nMonday, only Barber county currently reaches the 105 line. This \nwill be monitored for chances, especially if the ensembles \ncontinue to be a couple degrees too cool in this pattern. By \nTuesday morning, ensembles have precipitation chances at around \n10-20% in the western counties. With the low chances and \nuncertainty, not much more information can be gleaned from the \nsignal.",
